Day 11 Documentation – Nginx Web Server & Static Website Hosting on AWS EC2

Aim

To install the Nginx web server on an Ubuntu EC2 instance, understand the web root directory, host a static website, and manage web server files and services.


---

Experiment 1: Install Nginx

Command

sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx -y

Purpose

apt update downloads the latest package information from repositories.

apt install nginx installs the Nginx web server.



---

What is Nginx?

Nginx is an open-source web server that serves web pages to users over HTTP and HTTPS. It is widely used because it is lightweight, fast, and capable of handling thousands of simultaneous connections.

Uses of Nginx

Web server

Reverse proxy

Load balancer

Static file hosting

API gateway



---

Experiment 2: Verify Nginx Service

Command

sudo systemctl status nginx

Output

Active: active (running)

Explanation

This confirms that the Nginx service is running successfully.


---

Experiment 3: Open the Website

Open:

http://Public-IP

You successfully saw:

Welcome to nginx!

This means:

EC2 instance is running.

Nginx is installed.

Port 80 is open.

Security Group allows HTTP traffic.

The web server is functioning correctly.



---

Experiment 4: Understand the Web Root Directory

Move into the Nginx web directory:

cd /var/www/html

Check files:

ls -l

Output:

index.nginx-debian.html


---

What is /var/www/html?

/var/www/html is the default document root of Nginx in Ubuntu.

A document root is the directory from which the web server serves files to users.

When someone visits:

http://Public-IP

Nginx automatically searches for:

/var/www/html/index.html

or

/var/www/html/index.nginx-debian.html

and displays that file in the browser.


---

Experiment 5: Error Faced

You entered:

cd var/www/html

Error:

No such file or directory

Reason

You forgot the leading slash.

Incorrect:

cd var/www/html

Correct:

cd /var/www/html


---

Relative Path vs Absolute Path

Relative Path

var/www/html

Starts from the current directory.

Absolute Path

/var/www/html

Starts from the root directory (/).

Cloud engineers generally use absolute paths while administering servers.


---

Experiment 6: Create a Custom Web Page

Create a webpage:

sudo nano /var/www/html/index.html

Example:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>My First AWS Website</title>
</head>
<body>
<h1>Hello AWS!</h1>
<h2>Welcome to my Cloud Engineer journey.</h2>
</body>
</html>

Save:

Ctrl + O

Enter

Ctrl + X



---

Experiment 7: Verify Website Files

ls -l /var/www/html

Example:

-rw-r--r-- index.html
-rw-r--r-- index.nginx-debian.html

This confirms that the webpage exists inside the web root.


---

Experiment 8: Restart Nginx

sudo systemctl restart nginx

Purpose

Reloads the web server and applies new changes.


---

Experiment 9: Access the Website

Open:

http://Public-IP

Your custom webpage should now appear instead of the default Nginx page.


---

Nginx Service Management Commands

Start Nginx:

sudo systemctl start nginx

Stop Nginx:

sudo systemctl stop nginx

Restart Nginx:

sudo systemctl restart nginx

Check status:

sudo systemctl status nginx

Enable automatic startup:

sudo systemctl enable nginx


---

Nginx Logs

Access logs:

sudo tail -20 /var/log/nginx/access.log

Error logs:

sudo tail -20 /var/log/nginx/error.log

Purpose

Logs help Cloud Engineers troubleshoot website failures and monitor traffic.


---

Security Group Requirement

For users to access the website:

Type	Port	Source

HTTP	80	0.0.0.0/0


Without this rule, the website cannot be accessed from the browser.


